Warnings go to stderr as each stage produces them and change nothing
else: the document is still written and the exit code is still 0, so a
warning cannot break a build used as a CI gate. Every case is here at
once — an imported file's own `service`, a `template defaults` nothing
applies, a `$param` inside a template's string that isn't a parameter
reference, and a declared network no service references — which also
pins the order they print in: linking, then composition, then codegen,
the order the stages run.

The generated document is checked by `warnings.out/`, which is what
holds the second half of this: warnings did not stop the build.

```console
$ hllc build web.hll --out docker-compose.yml
lib.hll:1:10: warning: template `defaults` is not applied to anything — it is an ordinary template now, no longer applied implicitly to every service; add `with defaults` to each service that wants it
lib.hll:5:9: warning: service `db` is declared in an imported file and is not compiled — only the entry file's services are built
lib.hll:11:43: warning: `$host` inside a string is not a parameter reference — it is emitted verbatim; write `{{host}}` to interpolate template `rule`'s `host` here
web.hll:3:9: warning: network `unused` is declared but no service references it, so it is not emitted — add it to a service's `networks [...]` list, or remove the declaration
docker-compose.yml

```
